                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Accessibility](/docs/guide/accessibility)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("One component, side by side", id: "one-component-side-by-side").h2

        Markdown("Here is the same counter in each framework: it starts at a number the caller passes, counts up on a click, shows a badge when the count is even, and tells the caller about each change.\n\nReact:")

        CodeBlock(#"function Counter({ start = 0, onChange }) {
  const [n, setN] = useState(start);
  const even = useMemo(() => n % 2 === 0, [n]);
  return (
    <div className="row">
      <button onClick={() => { setN(n + 1); onChange?.(n + 1); }}>+</button>
      <span>{n}</span>
      {even && <span className="badge">even</span>}
    </div>
  );
}"#, title: "jsx")

        Markdown("Vue:")

        CodeBlock(#"<script setup>
import { ref, computed } from "vue";
const props = defineProps({ start: { type: Number, default: 0 } });
const emit = defineEmits(["change"]);
const n = ref(props.start);
const even = computed(() => n.value % 2 === 0);
function inc() { n.value++; emit("change", n.value); }
</script>

<template>
  <div class="row">
    <button @click="inc">+</button>
    <span>{{ n }}</span>
    <span v-if="even" class="badge">even</span>
  </div>
</template>"#, title: "vue")

        Markdown("Svelte:")

        CodeBlock(#"<script>
  let { start = 0, onchange } = $props();
  let n = $state(start);
  let even = $derived(n % 2 === 0);
  function inc() { n += 1; onchange?.(n); }
</script>

<div class="row">
  <button onclick={inc}>+</button>
  <span>{n}</span>
  {#if even}<span class="badge">even</span>{/if}
</div>"#, title: "svelte")

        Markdown("WebFluent:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Counter(start: Number = 0) {
    event change(value: Number)
    state n = start
    derived even = n % 2 == 0
    Row(gap: .sm, align: .center) {
        Button("+") { on click { n = n + 1  emit change(n) } }
        Text("{n}")
        if even { Badge("even").info }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("What changed:\n\n- **No hooks and no dependency arrays.** `derived` knows what it read.\n- **No setter.** You assign: `n = n + 1`.\n- **No `className`.** `Badge` comes designed, and `.info` is a flag the compiler checks.\n- **The event is declared.** A caller that handles `on chnage` gets error `E103` at build time, instead of a handler that never runs.\n\nA page places the counter and handles its event like this:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Scores(path: "/", title: "Scores", description: "A counter that reports each change.") {
    state last = 0
    Heading("Scores").h1
    Counter(start: 2) { on change(value) { last = value } }
    Text("Last change: {last}")
}

component Counter(start: Number = 0) {
    event change(value: Number)
    state n = start
    derived even = n % 2 == 0
    Row(gap: .sm, align: .center) {
        Button("+") { on click { n = n + 1  emit change(n) } }
        Text("{n}")
        if even { Badge("even").info }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The page shows `2`, the **even** badge and `Last change: 0`. After one click it shows `3`, no badge, and `Last change: 3`.")

        Heading("What works differently", id: "what-works-differently").h2

        Markdown("**It is a compiler, not a library.** There is no virtual DOM and no re-render of a component. Each text, attribute or style that reads a state subscribes to it and updates on its own. A component's body runs once, when the component is placed.\n\n**The markup is typed.** The compiler knows the props, flags, events and slots of every built-in, and of every component you declare. `Button(\"Save\").primray` does not build. Unknown props do not pass through to the DOM silently:\n\n- On a built-in, a named argument it does not declare is written to the element as an HTML attribute, with warning `C04` — unless it is `aria-*`, `data-*` or a global attribute such as `id`, `role` or `title`, which draw no warning.\n- On your own component, a named argument it does not declare is error `C02`. The exceptions are the same attribute families (`aria-*`, `data-*`, `id`, `role`, `title`, `hidden`), which go to the component's root element.\n\n**Pages own their routes.** There is no route table and no file-system routing. Each `page` names its `path:`, and the compiler builds the router from them, ordered by specificity, so the order you declare pages in cannot hide a route.\n\n**No imports.** Every file under `src/` is part of one program. A component declared anywhere is usable everywhere, and two declarations with one name are an error.\n\n**Styling lives on the element.** A `style \{ \}` block is real CSS scoped to that element, and `$token` reads a design token. There is no CSS-in-JS runtime: the build compiles static rules into the stylesheet.\n\n**The build is the site.** Pre-rendering, code splitting, precompression, image resizing, a sitemap, Open Graph tags and a Content-Security-Policy all come from `wf build`, with no plugins to choose.\n\n**Safe by construction.** Text is always text. The only way to put markup on a page is `Unsafe.Html(…)`, which the build flags every time. Inline handlers and `javascript:` URLs do not compile. See [Security](/docs/guide/security).")

        Heading("Things you might look for", id: "things-you-might-look-for").h2

        Markdown("- **npm packages.** There is no package manager for WebFluent code. A JavaScript library is a URL in `meta.scripts`. Your own JavaScript is a plain `.js` file under `src/`, and `.wf` code calls its functions by name. `mount:` and `cleanup:` hand any element to a script for as long as the element exists. [JavaScript interop](/docs/guide/interop) shows a chart library end to end.\n- **Server components, or rendering on every request.** WebFluent does not do this. A WebFluent site is static files plus a browser runtime, served by any host and talking to your API. For HTML generated on a server, such as emails, invoices and fragments, see [Server rendering](/docs/guide/server-rendering).\n- **WebFluent inside an existing React or Vue app.** Publish components as custom elements and place their tags: [Custom elements](/docs/guide/interop#publish-your-components-as-custom-elements).\n- **HTMX.** The closest thing to `hx-get` is a `resource` whose URL reads state, and the closest thing to `hx-post` is an action that `await`s an `api` endpoint. The page stays static HTML until its script arrives, then updates in place.")
    }
}
